# =============================================================================
# .gitignore for hao-backprop-test
# =============================================================================
# This is a minimal Python 3 / Flask HTTP server project. Historically this
# repository hosted a Node.js implementation which was fully migrated to
# Python/Flask; the Node.js artifacts (server.js, package.json,
# package-lock.json) were deleted as part of that completed migration.
#
# This .gitignore file serves THREE purposes:
#   1. (PRIMARY) Defensive guardrail — prevent accidental reintroduction of
#      Node.js artifacts by tools like `npm init`, IDE project wizards, or
#      JavaScript scaffolders. See AAP Section 0.4.1 Layer 3.
#   2. Ignore standard Python build/test artifacts (bytecode, __pycache__,
#      pytest cache, coverage reports, virtual environments).
#   3. Ignore per-contributor IDE / OS metadata that should not be shared.
#
# Traceability:
#   - AAP Section 0.2.2  — Secondary Root Cause: Absence of Defensive Guardrails
#   - AAP Section 0.3.2  — Repository File Analysis (confirmed .gitignore absent)
#   - AAP Section 0.3.3  — Case C: accidental future reintroduction mitigation
#   - AAP Section 0.4.1  — Layer 3: Reintroduction guardrail (defensive hardening)
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 1: Node.js reintroduction guardrails (CRITICAL — primary purpose)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These patterns prevent the accidental reintroduction of legacy Node.js
# artifacts that were removed during the completed Node.js → Python/Flask
# migration. See AAP Section 0.2.2 (Secondary Root Cause) and Section 0.4.1
# Layer 3. Without these entries, git would silently track regenerated Node.js
# artifacts produced by commands such as `npm init`, `npm install`, IDE
# project wizards, or JavaScript scaffolders — reintroducing the exact
# documentation-drift/tooling-misclassification bug this fix resolves.
#
# Historical context: package.json was deleted in commit a8bf8c0 and
# package-lock.json was deleted in commit 220d211 (both 2026-03-25) as part
# of the completed Node.js → Python/Flask migration. The patterns below
# cover every Node.js-tooling-generated file that would otherwise be auto-
# created in the repository root by npm or Yarn.

# npm-installed dependency directory — auto-created by `npm install`.
node_modules/

# npm package manifest — auto-created by `npm init` or IDE wizards.
# This is the EXACT file deleted per commit a8bf8c0 during the migration.
package.json

# npm dependency lockfile — auto-created by `npm install`.
# This is the EXACT file deleted per commit 220d211 during the migration.
package-lock.json

# npm error/debug logs — auto-created by npm when a command fails.
npm-debug.log*

# Yarn package manager lockfile — parallel to npm's package-lock.json.
yarn.lock

# Yarn debug log — created by `yarn` on failures.
yarn-debug.log*

# Yarn error log — created by `yarn` on failures.
yarn-error.log*

# Yarn Plug'n'Play runtime artifacts (Yarn 2+ zero-installs).
.pnp.*

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 2: Python bytecode and import cache
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Standard Python bytecode and import cache artifacts that should never be
# committed. These are regenerated automatically by the Python interpreter
# (CPython) whenever a module is imported and should remain developer-local.

# Python bytecode cache directory — CPython auto-creates one per package.
__pycache__/

# Compiled Python bytecode files (.pyc), optimized (.pyo), and pyd extensions.
*.py[cod]

# Jython class files.
*$py.class

# Compiled shared object extensions (C extensions, Cython output).
*.so

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 3: Python distribution / packaging artifacts
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build artifacts from Python packaging tools (setup.py, build, wheel,
# setuptools). These directories and files are produced by running package
# build commands (e.g., `python -m build`, `python setup.py sdist bdist_wheel`)
# and must never be tracked, as they are regenerated at release time.

# Legacy Python installer marker file.
.Python

# Generic build output directory.
build/

# setuptools develop-mode egg cache.
develop-eggs/

# Final distribution output (wheels, tarballs).
dist/

# Downloaded source artifacts cache.
downloads/

# setuptools egg cache.
eggs/

# Modern setuptools egg cache.
.eggs/

# setuptools-installed library paths (legacy layout).
lib/
lib64/

# zc.buildout parts.
parts/

# Source distribution output.
sdist/

# Variable-state directory used by buildout.
var/

# Wheel build output.
wheels/

# setuptools egg metadata directory.
*.egg-info/

# setuptools installation marker.
.installed.cfg

# Standalone egg files.
*.egg

# setuptools MANIFEST file (auto-generated from MANIFEST.in).
MANIFEST

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 4: pytest and coverage artifacts
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pytest and coverage.py artifacts generated when running the test suite
# (pytest per pytest.ini; pytest-cov per requirements-test.txt). The repo
# already contains .coverage and .pytest_cache/ artifacts after test runs
# that should not be tracked — these patterns ensure they are ignored.

# pytest cache directory (test collection metadata, last-failed tracking).
.pytest_cache/

# coverage.py binary data file.
.coverage

# coverage.py parallel-run data files.
.coverage.*

# HTML coverage report output directory.
htmlcov/

# Cobertura-style XML coverage report.
coverage.xml

# Line-level coverage reports.
*.cover

# Hypothesis (property-based testing) database.
.hypothesis/

# nose test runner XML reports (legacy).
nosetests.xml

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 5: Python virtual environments
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python virtual environment directories — developers may create these
# locally with various tools (venv, virtualenv, pyenv-virtualenv). Virtual
# environments are large, platform-specific, and reproducible from
# requirements.txt / requirements-test.txt, so they must never be committed.

# PEP 405 standard venv directory (hidden variant).
.venv/

# Standard venv directory used by this repo's setup.
venv/

# Uppercase ENV directory (historical convention).
ENV/

# Lowercase env directory (historical convention).
env/

# Backup environment directories created by tool-assisted upgrades.
env.bak/
venv.bak/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 6: IDE and editor local configuration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor / IDE local configuration and workspace files that vary per
# contributor. These settings are personal preferences (window layouts,
# run configurations, local indexes) and do not belong in shared source
# control.

# JetBrains IDEs (PyCharm, IntelliJ IDEA, WebStorm, etc.).
.idea/

# Visual Studio Code workspace settings.
.vscode/

# Vim swap files (created while editing).
*.swp
*.swo

# Generic backup files from many editors.
*~

# Eclipse project metadata.
.project

# PyDev (Eclipse Python plugin) project metadata.
.pydevproject

# Spyder IDE project metadata (older format).
.spyderproject

# Spyder IDE project metadata (newer format).
.spyproject

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 7: Operating-system metadata
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operating-system metadata files that have no place in source control.
# These are auto-generated by macOS Finder, Windows Explorer, and similar
# shells whenever a directory is browsed; they pollute commits with
# machine-specific state.

# macOS Finder metadata file.
.DS_Store

# macOS Finder metadata variant.
.DS_Store?

# macOS AppleDouble resource-fork files.
._*

# macOS Spotlight index.
.Spotlight-V100

# macOS Trash metadata.
.Trashes

# Windows Explorer thumbnail cache (variant).
ehthumbs.db

# Windows Explorer thumbnail cache.
Thumbs.db

# Windows folder customization.
Desktop.ini

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 8: Local environment / secrets
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local environment variable files that may contain secrets — never commit.
# These files typically hold API keys, database passwords, and other
# credentials that must remain outside version control.

# Primary dotenv file.
.env

# Local override dotenv.
.env.local

# Environment-specific local overrides (.env.development.local, etc.).
.env.*.local

# PEM-format keys and certificates.
*.pem

# Private key files.
*.key

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section 9: Miscellaneous
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Other commonly-ignored artifacts that show up across many projects —
# generic log files, temporary files, and editor/merge backup files.

# Generic log files from any tool.
*.log

# Temporary files.
*.tmp

# Generic backup files.
*.bak

# Merge/patch backup files (e.g., from `patch`, `git mergetool`).
*.orig
